- `create NAME [REV]` makes a new local bookmark at REV, `@` by default, as `jj bookmark create`
  does. A name in use is refused, `move` being for that. Nothing is pushed.
- `move NAME TARGET` points an existing local bookmark at one commit, `jj bookmark set NAME -r
  TARGET`, the repositioning a bookmark needs before it is pushed, since `push` only sets the line's
  bookmark to `@-`. The target is a revset of exactly one commit, a bookmark or a rev. A move
  backward or sideways, leaving commits off the bookmark's line, is refused unless
  `--allow-backwards`, as `jj` refuses it, and a bookmark already at the target is left as it is.
  Nothing is pushed.
- `rename OLD NEW` gives a local bookmark a new name at the commit it is at, as `jj bookmark rename`
  does, OLD missing or NEW in use refused. A published OLD stays on `origin` under its old name, and
  is said so, unless `--push`, which pushes NEW and then deletes OLD there, a publish taking its own
  approval.
- `delete NAME` deletes one bookmark locally and on `origin`, the disposal a cycle's bookmark gets
  at Land, `jj bookmark delete` and its push in one invocation. The remote goes first, so a failed
  push leaves the local bookmark, and a side already gone is no error, so a rerun finishes the job.
  The commits stay, and the commit the bookmark was at is printed so it can be recreated.
